Dominant error classes:
  li.3  : LI spacing < 0.17 um  (LI interconnect too close between devices)
  li.1  : LI width < 0.17 um    (LI wires too narrow)
  li.c1 : LI core width < 0.14 um
  li.6  : LI minimum area < 0.0561 um^2
  diff/tap.1: diffusion width < 0.15 um (device fingers too narrow)
  poly.1a: poly width < 0.15 um
  poly.4 : poly spacing to diffusion < 0.075 um (touching, not crossing)
  nwell.4: nwell has no metal-connected N+ tap
  LU.2/LU.3: tap distance rule (> 15 um)
  met1.1 / met1.6: metal1 width/area (bias cell)

Root cause: The scripted layout generator (TCL scripts 01_-07_.tcl) drew
geometry in Magic internal units (1 unit = 5 nm lambda/2) without
satisfying minimum dimension rules. A 1-unit rectangle is 5 nm, which
violates almost every minimum-width rule in sky130. The real intended
device dimensions (W=4u, L=2u etc.) were placed as bounding-box
coordinates scaled to Magic internal units, but the finger widths and
LI routing were not converted to the correct scale.

======================================================================
STAGE 2: LVS
======================================================================
Tool: Magic ext2spice + Netgen 1.5 lvs

Extraction result (Magic ext2spice, sky130A.tech, LVS style):
  ALL BLOCKS: 0 FET devices extracted

Root cause: In every cell, poly is placed ADJACENT to (touching)
diffusion at the boundary (0 nm overlap). Magic's extractor requires
poly to CROSS THROUGH diff to form a MOSFET channel region. Without
overlap, no 'fet' lines are generated in the .ext file, and the
output SPICE contains only an empty subcircuit header.

Netgen invocation:
  netgen -batch lvs "cp_pmos_array_layout.spice cp_pmos_array" \
    "sky130_cp.sp sky130_cp" sky130A_setup.tcl lvs_cp_pmos.rpt
  netgen -batch lvs "sky130_vco_layout_layout.spice sky130_vco_layout" \
    "sky130_vco.sp sky130_vco" sky130A_setup.tcl lvs_vco.rpt

Netgen output:
